\begin_layout Section
Foundations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Sets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Basic notions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
In mathematics,
 a set is a collection of different things,
 called elements or members of the set.
 A set may also be called a 
\emph on
collection
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
family
\emph default
,
 especially when its elements are themselves sets;
 this may avoid confusion between the set and its members.
 A set may be specified either by listing its elements or by giving a property that characterizes its elements,
 such as for the set of the prime numbers or the set of all students in a given class.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is an element of a set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 one says that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 belongs to 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 or 
\emph on
is in
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 and one writes 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in A$
\end_inset

.
 The statement 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is not in 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset


\emph default
 is written a 
\begin_inset Formula $x\notin a$
\end_inset

.
 Type notation can be used for affirmative membership,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x:A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
axiom of extensionality
\emph default
 states that two sets are equal if and only if they have the same elements,
 or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A=B\Longleftrightarrow\forall x(x\in A\Longrightarrow x\in B).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
There exists a set with no elements,
 and extensionality implies that there is only one such set.
 It is called the 
\emph on
empty set
\emph default
 (or null set) and is denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\varnothing$
\end_inset

 or {}.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A singleton is a set with exactly one element.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is this element,
 the singleton is denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x\}$
\end_inset

.
 The sets 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\varnothing\}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\varnothing$
\end_inset

 are different,
 because the former has one element (namely 
\begin_inset Formula $\varnothing$
\end_inset

) and the latter has no elements at all.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Specifying a set
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Extensionality implies that to specify a set,
 it suffices either to list its elements or to provide a property that characterize the set's elements among the elements of a possibly larger set.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Roster notation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\emph on
Roster
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
enumeration notation
\emph default
 is a notation introduced by Ernst Zermelo in 1908 that specifies a set by listing its elements between braces,
 separated by commas.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
The notations 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\}$
\end_inset

 for the empty set and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x\}$
\end_inset

 for a singleton are examples of roster notation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When specifying a set,
 all that matters is whether each potential element is in the set or not,
 so a set does not change if elements are repeated or arranged in a different order.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When there is a clear pattern for generating all set elements,
 one can use an ellipsis to abbreviate the notation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Set–builder notation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Set–builder notation specifies a set as being the set of all elements that satisfy some logical formula.
 More precisely,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\varpi(x)$
\end_inset

 is a logical formula depending on a variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 which evalutates to 
\begin_inset Formula $\top$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\bot$
\end_inset

 depending on the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\{x:\varpi(x)\}
\]

\end_inset

denotes the set of all 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 for which 
\begin_inset Formula $\varpi(x)$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\top$
\end_inset

.
 In this notation,
 
\begin_inset Formula $:$
\end_inset

 is read as 
\emph on
such that
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Set relations and operations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two types of things one can do with sets:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One can compare sets and see their relationship.
 These are called 
\emph on
set relations
\emph default
.
 Equality is the first such relation encountered this far.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One can produce new sets from other sets.
 These are called 
\emph on
set operations
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Subsets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As seen before,
 sets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 are equal iff they have the same elements,
 denoted as 
\begin_inset Formula $A=B$
\end_inset

.
 Equality is reflexive;
 for every set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $B=B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
subset
\emph default
 of a set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is a set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 such that every element of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is also an element of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\subseteq B\Longleftrightarrow\forall x(x\in A\Longrightarrow x\in B).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The relationship between sets established by 
\begin_inset Formula $\subseteq$
\end_inset

 is called inclusion or containment.
 Containment is reflexive too;
 every set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is a subset of itself,
 namely 
\begin_inset Formula $B\subseteq B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is a 
\emph on
proper subset
\emph default
 of a set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subseteq B$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A\neq B$
\end_inset

;
 to denote this one writes 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subset B$
\end_inset

,
 therefore
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\subset B\Longleftrightarrow A\subseteq B\wedge A\neq B.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Properties of containment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Containment is antisymmetic;
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall A,B$
\end_inset

 set,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A=B\Longleftrightarrow A\subseteq B\wedge A\supseteq B.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The empy set is a subset of every set:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall A$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varnothing\subseteq A$
\end_inset

.
 In particular,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall A\neq\varnothing$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varnothing\subset A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Basic-operations"

\end_inset

Basic operations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are several standard operations that produce new sets from given sets,
 analogously to how addition and multiplication produce new numbers from given numbers.
 The operations that are considered in this section are those such that all elements of the produced sets belong to a previously defined set.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Intersection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The 
\emph on
intersection
\emph default
 of sets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is set denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cap B$
\end_inset

 whose elements are those elements that belong to both 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 That is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\cap B=\{x:x\in A\wedge x\in B\},
\]

\end_inset

Apparently,
 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cap B\subseteq A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cap B\subseteq B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Intersection is associative and commutative;
 this means that for proceeding a sequence of intersections,
 one may proceed in any order,
 without the need of parentheses for specifying the order of operations,
 for example,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall A,B,C$
\end_inset

 sets:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(A\cap B)\cap C=A\cap(B\cap C)=A\cap B\cap C=\{x|x\in A\wedge x\in B\wedge x\in C\}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

 is a nonempty set of sets,
 its intersection,
 denoted
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bigcap\nolimits\mathcal{S}=\bigcap\nolimits_{A\in\mathcal{S}}A,
\]

\end_inset

is the set whose elements are those elements that belond to all sets in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

.
 That is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bigcap\nolimits\mathcal{S}=\Big\{ x\Big|\bigwedge\nolimits_{A\in\mathcal{S}}(x\in A)\Big\}=\{x|(\forall A\in\mathcal{S})x\in A\}.
\]

\end_inset

Example:
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}=\{X,Y,Z\}$
\end_inset

,
 then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bigcap\nolimits\mathcal{S}=X\cap Y\cap Z.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Union
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The 
\emph on
union
\emph default
 of two sets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is a set denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cup B$
\end_inset

 whose elements are those elements that belong to 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 or both.
 That is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\cup B=\{x|x\in A\vee x\in B\},
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\vee$
\end_inset

 denotes the logical 
\family typewriter
or
\family default
.
 Apparently,
 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subseteq A\cup B$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B\subseteq A\cup B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Union is associative and commutative.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

 is a set of sets,
 its union,
 denoted
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bigcup\mathcal{S}=\bigcup_{A\in\mathcal{S}}A,
\]

\end_inset

is the set whose elements are those elementes that belong to at least one set in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

.
 That is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bigcup\mathcal{S}=\Big\{ x\Big|\bigvee\nolimits_{A\in\mathcal{S}}(x\in A)\Big\}=\{x|(\exists A\in\mathcal{S})x\in A\}.
\]

\end_inset

Example:
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}=\{X,Y,Z\}$
\end_inset

,
 then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bigcup\mathcal{S}=X\cup Y\cup Z.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Set difference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The 
\emph on
set difference
\emph default
 of two sets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 is a set,
 denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $A\setminus B$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $A-B$
\end_inset

,
 whose elements are those elements that belong to 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 but not to 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 That is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\setminus B=\{x|x\in A\wedge x\notin B\}.
\]

\end_inset

Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $A\setminus B=A\setminus(A\cap B)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $B\subseteq A$
\end_inset

 the difference 
\begin_inset Formula $A\setminus B=\neg_{A}B$
\end_inset

 is also called the 
\emph on
complement
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 Naively speaking,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\neg B=\{x|x\notin B\},
\]

\end_inset

but this does not lead to a set as is.
 When all sets that are considered are subsets of a fixed 
\emph on
universal set
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

,
 the complement 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg_{U}A$
\end_inset

 is often called the absolute complement of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 and is denoted simply as 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The 
\emph on
symmetric difference
\emph default
 of two sets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $A\bigtriangleup B$
\end_inset

,
 is the set of those elements that belong to 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 but not on both:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\bigtriangleup B=(A\setminus B)\cup(B\setminus A)=(A\cup B)\setminus(A\cap B).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset line
LatexCommand rule
offset "0.5ex"
width "100col%"
height "1pt"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mathematical sets do not exactly correspong to types in the object–oriented programming paradigm,
 however 
\begin_inset listings
